CAT = "mlsImmuno"; SEG = "IMM"
Q = [
('Hemolytic disease of the fetus and newborn is which type of hypersensitivity?',
 'Type II, cytotoxic antibody', ['Type I, IgE mediated', 'Type III, immune complex', 'Type IV, T cell mediated'],
 'Maternal IgG binds fetal red cell antigens and leads to their destruction, a type II reaction. Type III reactions come from circulating immune complexes.'),
('A low C3 with a normal C4 suggests complement activation through the:',
 'Alternative pathway', ['Classical pathway', 'Lectin pathway', 'C1 inhibitor loss'],
 'The alternative pathway uses C3 but bypasses C4. Classical and lectin activation consume C4 as well, so both values fall.'),
('Recurrent swelling without hives, with a low C4 and a normal C3, suggests deficiency of:',
 'C1 esterase inhibitor', ['Factor H', 'Decay-accelerating factor', 'Membrane attack complex'],
 'Without C1 inhibitor, C1 keeps cleaving C4 and bradykinin builds up, causing hereditary angioedema. Factor H deficiency lowers C3.'),
('Which autoantibody is the most specific for systemic lupus erythematosus?',
 'Anti-Sm', ['Anti-histone', 'Anti-SSA (Ro)', 'Anti-U1 RNP'],
 'Anti-Smith is found in a minority of SLE patients but is nearly specific for it. Anti-histone fits drug-induced lupus, and anti-U1 RNP fits mixed connective tissue disease.'),
('A patient taking procainamide has a positive ANA. The antibodies are most often directed against:',
 'Histones', ['Sm antigen', 'Topoisomerase I', 'Jo-1 (histidyl-tRNA synthetase)'],
 'Drug-induced lupus from procainamide or hydralazine typically has antihistone antibodies and spares the kidneys. Anti-topoisomerase I (Scl-70) points to diffuse systemic sclerosis.'),
('Which antibody is more specific than rheumatoid factor for rheumatoid arthritis?',
 'Anti-CCP antibody', ['Antinuclear antibody', 'Anti-streptolysin O', 'Anti-double-stranded DNA'],
 'Anti-CCP is highly specific for RA and can appear years before symptoms. Rheumatoid factor also occurs in other autoimmune diseases, infection and older age.'),
('A cytoplasmic ANCA pattern with antibody to proteinase 3 suggests:',
 'Granulomatosis with polyangiitis', ['Microscopic polyangiitis', 'Anti-GBM disease', 'Eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis'],
 'C-ANCA against PR3 is the classic finding in granulomatosis with polyangiitis. Perinuclear ANCA against myeloperoxidase is more typical of microscopic polyangiitis.'),
('Why is total IgA measured along with tissue transglutaminase IgA when testing for celiac disease?',
 'IgA deficiency gives false negatives', ['Total IgA confirms gluten intake', 'Low IgA proves active celiac disease', 'Total IgA replaces the biopsy'],
 'Selective IgA deficiency is more common in celiac disease, and it makes IgA-based tests falsely negative. IgG-based tests are used for those patients.'),
('A boy over 6 months old has recurrent bacterial infections, absent B cells and very low immunoglobulins. This suggests:',
 'X-linked agammaglobulinemia', ['DiGeorge syndrome', 'Selective IgA deficiency', 'Chronic granulomatous disease'],
 'BTK mutations block B cell maturation, and illness starts as maternal IgG wanes. DiGeorge syndrome affects T cells through thymic hypoplasia.'),
('Graft-versus-host disease after a stem cell transplant is caused by:',
 'Donor T cells attacking host tissue', ['Host T cells attacking the graft', 'Host antibodies to donor HLA', 'Donor B cells making autoantibodies'],
 "Immunocompetent donor T cells react to the recipient's HLA, damaging skin, liver and gut. Host cells attacking the graft is rejection."),
('Which HLA loci are class II?',
 'DR, DQ and DP', ['A, B and C', 'A, B and DR', 'E, F and G'],
 'Class II molecules are on antigen-presenting cells and present peptides to CD4 T cells. HLA-A, B and C are class I, found on nearly all nucleated cells.'),
('A reactive HIV-1/2 antigen/antibody test with a negative HIV-1/HIV-2 differentiation assay is followed by:',
 'HIV-1 nucleic acid test', ['HIV-1 Western blot', 'Repeat screening in a year', 'CD4 lymphocyte count'],
 'A reactive combination test with a negative antibody differentiation assay may be acute infection, when p24 antigen is present before antibodies. HIV RNA testing resolves it; Western blot is no longer used.'),
('HBsAg has stayed positive for 8 months with total anti-HBc positive and IgM anti-HBc negative. This indicates:',
 'Chronic hepatitis B', ['Acute hepatitis B', 'Resolved infection', 'Recent vaccination'],
 'HBsAg present for more than 6 months defines chronic infection, and a negative IgM anti-HBc argues against a recent one. Resolution would replace HBsAg with anti-HBs.'),
('Which hepatitis B result best measures how much virus is replicating?',
 'HBV DNA level', ['Anti-HBe level', 'Anti-HBs level', 'Total anti-HBc'],
 'Quantitative HBV DNA shows the viral load directly and guides treatment. Antibody markers show past or present immune response, not the amount of virus.'),
('A positive IgM Lyme result should be disregarded when the patient has been ill for more than:',
 '30 days', ['7 days', '6 months', '1 year'],
 'CDC advises ignoring a positive IgM result after more than 30 days of illness, because false positives are common then and true infection would have made IgG.'),
('In the reverse syphilis algorithm, a reactive treponemal EIA with a nonreactive RPR is followed by:',
 'A second treponemal test', ['Repeat RPR in one week only', 'Treatment with no more testing', 'VDRL on cerebrospinal fluid'],
 'A second treponemal test such as TP-PA decides whether the first was falsely reactive. If it is reactive, the patient has had syphilis at some time, treated or untreated.'),
]
